1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,977
In 1974, Canada walked out of a half-built

2
00:00:02,977 --> 00:00:06,592
nuclear reactor on the Chambal river, in
Rajasthan.

3
00:00:06,838 --> 00:00:08,086
India finished it.

4
00:00:08,286 --> 00:00:11,448
But 20 years later, its heart was wearing
out.

5
00:00:11,705 --> 00:00:14,291
306 thin metal tubes.

6
00:00:14,450 --> 00:00:17,565
Only one country had ever replaced tubes
like these.

7
00:00:17,799 --> 00:00:18,434
Canada.

8
00:00:18,638 --> 00:00:21,855
And Canada's export rules kept its
companies from helping.

9
00:00:22,044 --> 00:00:23,840
So how do you rebuild the heart of

10
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:26,216
a machine that nobody will teach you to
open?

11
00:00:26,455 --> 00:00:30,431
By the end, you will see Indian engineers
pull those tubes out,

12
00:00:30,431 --> 00:00:32,388
one by one, into a steel flask.

13
00:00:32,555 --> 00:00:35,455
And you will see why it matters for the
new reactor

14
00:00:35,455 --> 00:00:38,809
that began taking in fuel this September,
on the same lake.

15
00:00:39,105 --> 00:00:40,845
Let me take you to Rawatbhata.

16
00:00:41,023 --> 00:00:45,755
A small town on the Rana Pratap Sagar
lake, about 64 km from Kota.

17
00:00:46,003 --> 00:00:47,936
If you had come here in the 1960s,

18
00:00:47,936 --> 00:00:52,426
you would have seen Canada and India
building a nuclear power station together.

19
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:54,390
Its design came from Canada.

20
00:00:54,545 --> 00:00:55,857
And why that design?

21
00:00:55,997 --> 00:00:58,403
India had picked it for one simple reason.

22
00:00:58,514 --> 00:01:00,718
It needs no enriched uranium.

23
00:01:00,853 --> 00:01:05,443
You can run it on natural uranium, with
the same mix of atoms you find in nature.

24
00:01:05,565 --> 00:01:07,776
And Indian factories could build it.

25
00:01:07,959 --> 00:01:12,407
Now, you have probably seen a reactor
drawn as one giant steel pot.

26
00:01:12,582 --> 00:01:14,859
Most reactors in the world are just that.

27
00:01:15,018 --> 00:01:16,551
This one is different.

28
00:01:16,759 --> 00:01:20,343
Imagine a tank, pierced by 306 tubes.

29
00:01:20,492 --> 00:01:23,649
Each tube, if you like, is its own small
pressure cooker.

30
00:01:23,846 --> 00:01:25,369
Think of the cooker in your kitchen.

31
00:01:25,492 --> 00:01:28,007
You know that whistle, you have heard it
all your life.

32
00:01:28,197 --> 00:01:32,798
Inside, the steam pushes at about 1 kg on
every square centimetre.

33
00:01:32,932 --> 00:01:36,156
Now jump ahead to India's newest reactors
for a moment.

34
00:01:36,285 --> 00:01:42,432
There, the water in each tube is pushed
100 times harder, at 310 degrees.

35
00:01:42,561 --> 00:01:47,938
And in reactors of this family, the wall
of that tube is only about 4 mm thick.

36
00:01:48,060 --> 00:01:49,076
Think of a pencil.

37
00:01:49,148 --> 00:01:50,877
The wall is thinner than that.

38
00:01:51,068 --> 00:01:53,301
Then came May 1974.

39
00:01:53,434 --> 00:01:54,324
You remember it.

40
00:01:54,415 --> 00:01:55,031
Pokhran.

41
00:01:55,214 --> 00:01:56,965
Why did Canada care so much?

42
00:01:57,081 --> 00:01:59,104
India called its test peaceful.

43
00:01:59,244 --> 00:02:02,646
Canada saw plutonium from a research
reactor near Mumbai,

44
00:02:02,646 --> 00:02:05,332
called CIRUS, which Canada had helped
design.

45
00:02:05,469 --> 00:02:07,634
So Canada stopped its nuclear help.

46
00:02:07,727 --> 00:02:09,923
The engineers packed up and went home.

47
00:02:10,041 --> 00:02:11,701
The first unit was running.

48
00:02:11,822 --> 00:02:17,487
The second, as an Indian magazine put it
in 1996, the Canadians abandoned halfway.

49
00:02:17,750 --> 00:02:21,986
Now you might ask me, Kishore, so what
happened to your half-built reactor?

50
00:02:22,204 --> 00:02:24,516
Indian engineers finished it on their own.

51
00:02:24,644 --> 00:02:26,711
It went to work in 1981.

52
00:02:26,784 --> 00:02:28,687
And for a while, all was well.

53
00:02:28,739 --> 00:02:30,673
At Kaiga in Karnataka,

54
00:02:30,673 --> 00:02:36,301
a reactor of this family once ran for 962
days without stopping.

55
00:02:36,583 --> 00:02:37,650
A world record.

56
00:02:38,075 --> 00:02:40,273
So what stops a machine like that?

57
00:02:40,591 --> 00:02:42,227
You might guess the fuel.

58
00:02:42,630 --> 00:02:43,826
It is not the fuel.

59
00:02:44,048 --> 00:02:46,550
You can feed fresh fuel in while it runs.

60
00:02:46,875 --> 00:02:48,940
What wears out is the tubes.

61
00:02:49,260 --> 00:02:52,196
The early tubes were made of an older
metal mix.

62
00:02:52,383 --> 00:02:54,802
Over the years they soaked up hydrogen.

63
00:02:55,094 --> 00:02:59,145
NPCIL, the company that runs India's
nuclear plants,

64
00:02:59,145 --> 00:03:02,028
says they lasted only 10 to 12 years.

65
00:03:02,272 --> 00:03:07,080
In August 1983, near Toronto, the world
saw what that means.

66
00:03:07,329 --> 00:03:10,911
At the Pickering station, one tube broke
at full power.

67
00:03:11,189 --> 00:03:13,237
Just one, out of hundreds.

68
00:03:13,545 --> 00:03:15,388
But that is all it takes.

69
00:03:15,757 --> 00:03:17,923
How does a tube just break?

70
00:03:18,262 --> 00:03:21,024
Each hot tube sits inside a cooler tube.

71
00:03:21,176 --> 00:03:24,863
Small coiled springs, wrapped around it,
keep the two apart.

72
00:03:25,112 --> 00:03:28,412
In that one channel, a spring had slipped
out of place.

73
00:03:28,621 --> 00:03:30,875
Slowly, the hot tube sagged.

74
00:03:31,058 --> 00:03:33,029
Then it touched the cold one.

75
00:03:33,338 --> 00:03:36,161
Now think of a glass of cold water on a
hot day.

76
00:03:36,440 --> 00:03:39,695
You have seen the drops gather on the
outside, on the cold glass.

77
00:03:40,045 --> 00:03:44,178
Inside the metal, hydrogen did something
like the drops you just pictured.

78
00:03:44,376 --> 00:03:46,396
It crept to the coldest spot.

79
00:03:46,681 --> 00:03:49,784
And there it hardened into brittle
blisters.

80
00:03:50,018 --> 00:03:51,475
The blisters cracked.

81
00:03:51,624 --> 00:03:55,388
And the crack kept growing, until the tube
split.

82
00:03:55,529 --> 00:04:00,163
The operators, I should tell you, brought
the reactor safely to a stop.

83
00:04:00,189 --> 00:04:03,836
But every reactor of this kind now had a
question to answer.

84
00:04:04,043 --> 00:04:08,019
India's tubes at Rawatbhata were made of
the same older metal.

85
00:04:08,245 --> 00:04:10,052
And as Business India reported,

86
00:04:10,052 --> 00:04:13,824
Indian engineers were denied detailed
knowledge of the accident,

87
00:04:13,824 --> 00:04:15,298
its cause and the remedy.

88
00:04:15,621 --> 00:04:17,947
So India had the same disease.

89
00:04:18,165 --> 00:04:19,625
And no doctor would come.

90
00:04:19,974 --> 00:04:21,141
What would you have done?

91
00:04:21,940 --> 00:04:26,324
In 1995, a nuclear trade weekly reported
India's decision.

92
00:04:26,553 --> 00:04:31,494
Rajasthan unit 2 would get all new tubes,
with or without outside help.

93
00:04:31,740 --> 00:04:34,504
India was still a member of the Kan-doo
Owners Group,

94
00:04:34,504 --> 00:04:37,215
the club of countries that run this kind
of reactor.

95
00:04:37,336 --> 00:04:41,641
But Canada's export rules kept Canadian
firms from supplying the tools,

96
00:04:41,641 --> 00:04:42,672
or doing the job.

97
00:04:42,946 --> 00:04:44,552
So the job came home.

98
00:04:44,788 --> 00:04:48,019
The project director at Rawatbhata was V K
Chaturvedi.

99
00:04:48,218 --> 00:04:51,767
The team that would change the tubes was
led by R C Arya.

100
00:04:52,001 --> 00:04:53,692
Can you picture the hardest part?

101
00:04:53,873 --> 00:04:59,187
Every one of the 306 tubes had been inside
a running reactor for years.

102
00:04:59,365 --> 00:05:00,885
You cannot stand near it.

103
00:05:00,967 --> 00:05:02,128
Not for a minute.

104
00:05:02,294 --> 00:05:07,256
So every cut, every pull, every weld had
to be done by machines.

105
00:05:07,382 --> 00:05:09,773
Machines that did not exist yet.

106
00:05:09,902 --> 00:05:11,575
Indian engineers designed them.

107
00:05:11,805 --> 00:05:13,885
Now picture one old tube with me.

108
00:05:14,083 --> 00:05:16,298
A tool reaches in and cuts it free.

109
00:05:16,483 --> 00:05:20,449
The tube is drawn, slowly, into a heavy
steel flask.

110
00:05:20,548 --> 00:05:22,905
The flask carries it away to a vault.

111
00:05:23,044 --> 00:05:25,643
You will not see a single person beside
it.

112
00:05:25,807 --> 00:05:27,796
Everything is done from a distance.

113
00:05:27,939 --> 00:05:30,801
Then, as you watch, a new tube goes in.

114
00:05:30,890 --> 00:05:34,785
This time, made of a stronger mix,
zirconium with niobium.

115
00:05:34,921 --> 00:05:35,983
And then the next.

116
00:05:36,074 --> 00:05:38,090
306 times.

117
00:05:38,292 --> 00:05:39,682
And what did all this cost?

118
00:05:39,780 --> 00:05:44,392
Going by Business India's figures, turned
into rupees at that year's rate,

119
00:05:44,392 --> 00:05:46,885
India planned to spend about ₹250 crore.

120
00:05:47,017 --> 00:05:50,858
Canada had spent more than ₹1,000 crore on
the same kind of job.

121
00:05:51,007 --> 00:05:51,766
Was it quick?

122
00:05:51,920 --> 00:05:52,362
No.

123
00:05:52,520 --> 00:05:57,337
The reactor made no power in 1995, 1996 or
1997.

124
00:05:57,416 --> 00:06:02,740
By 1998, if you drove past Rawatbhata, you
would have seen unit 2 running again.

125
00:06:02,912 --> 00:06:08,088
NPCIL calls it the first such job in
India, and only the second in the world.

126
00:06:08,213 --> 00:06:10,154
The first was in Canada itself.

127
00:06:10,397 --> 00:06:14,808
Bhabha Atomic Research Centre later
counted what tools like these gave back.

128
00:06:14,950 --> 00:06:18,987
About ₹1,100 crore of extra electricity.

129
00:06:19,075 --> 00:06:21,861
You remember how we never threw away a
broken radio?

130
00:06:21,990 --> 00:06:23,031
We opened it.

131
00:06:23,139 --> 00:06:25,201
That is the lesson of Rawatbhata.

132
00:06:25,290 --> 00:06:29,609
A machine is truly yours only when you can
open it, and mend it.

133
00:06:29,731 --> 00:06:32,404
If you want the next machine opened like
this,

134
00:06:32,404 --> 00:06:34,729
subscribe, so the next one comes to you.

135
00:06:34,843 --> 00:06:35,615
On the TV?

136
00:06:35,771 --> 00:06:37,617
Just press Subscribe with your remote.

137
00:06:37,740 --> 00:06:39,223
But I must be fair with you.

138
00:06:39,314 --> 00:06:42,495
You may have heard people say these tubes
are dangerous.

139
00:06:42,618 --> 00:06:44,216
Is it safe to live near one?

140
00:06:44,385 --> 00:06:46,161
Let me show you the worst day.

141
00:06:46,351 --> 00:06:48,590
The 11 March, 2016.

142
00:06:48,683 --> 00:06:50,243
Kakrapar, in Gujarat.

143
00:06:50,319 --> 00:06:53,268
At about 9 in the morning, a tube began to
leak.

144
00:06:53,377 --> 00:06:56,510
The reactor shut itself down, the way you
would want it to.

145
00:06:56,631 --> 00:06:58,256
There was no fuel failure.

146
00:06:58,372 --> 00:07:01,973
The regulator said there was no radiation
harm to the workers,

147
00:07:01,973 --> 00:07:04,760
to the people outside, or to the land
around it.

148
00:07:04,939 --> 00:07:06,770
So how serious was it?

149
00:07:06,963 --> 00:07:10,344
The international scale rated it two, out
of seven.

150
00:07:10,511 --> 00:07:13,041
An incident, not an accident.

151
00:07:13,271 --> 00:07:14,290
What went wrong?

152
00:07:14,436 --> 00:07:17,583
A wrong chemical had got into the gas
around the tubes.

153
00:07:17,665 --> 00:07:20,561
It had eaten small spots into their outer
walls.

154
00:07:20,693 --> 00:07:22,252
And was it everywhere?

155
00:07:22,443 --> 00:07:22,949
No.

156
00:07:23,098 --> 00:07:26,414
The regulator found it only in Kakrapar's
two units.

157
00:07:26,476 --> 00:07:30,758
So, as you might expect, Kakrapar did what
Rawatbhata had taught.

158
00:07:30,922 --> 00:07:32,476
Every tube came out.

159
00:07:32,513 --> 00:07:34,815
306 in each reactor.

160
00:07:34,930 --> 00:07:38,983
A former chief of NPCIL put it in words
you will not forget.

161
00:07:39,110 --> 00:07:42,537
Replacing them is like a heart transplant
for a human being.

162
00:07:42,719 --> 00:07:45,529
And that was the start of a bigger story.

163
00:07:45,661 --> 00:07:49,052
Now look at the reactor face with me, and
count with your eyes.

164
00:07:49,156 --> 00:07:52,740
306 circles grow to 392.

165
00:07:52,889 --> 00:07:57,571
This is India's own 700 MW reactor,
designed here.

166
00:07:57,692 --> 00:08:00,286
Around each tube flows a dry gas.

167
00:08:00,360 --> 00:08:02,541
If even a drop of water leaks in,

168
00:08:02,541 --> 00:08:05,979
the gas turns damp, like the dew on your
cold glass.

169
00:08:06,074 --> 00:08:07,766
Remember that glass?

170
00:08:07,774 --> 00:08:09,548
Here, the dew is the alarm.

171
00:08:09,637 --> 00:08:13,846
The first one, at Kakrapar, went critical
in July 2020.

172
00:08:13,967 --> 00:08:14,622
Critical?

173
00:08:14,754 --> 00:08:16,068
That is good news here.

174
00:08:16,116 --> 00:08:19,573
It means the chain reaction has started,
and holds steady.

175
00:08:19,639 --> 00:08:22,229
Many of its tests were done during the
lockdown.

176
00:08:22,301 --> 00:08:25,605
And Rawatbhata, where this story began,
got its own.

177
00:08:25,692 --> 00:08:28,682
Unit 7 joined the grid in March 2025,

178
00:08:28,682 --> 00:08:32,480
and was running at full power by February
2026.

179
00:08:32,512 --> 00:08:36,133
And this September, on the same lake you
saw at the start,

180
00:08:36,133 --> 00:08:39,692
where Canada walked out, unit 8 began
taking in its fuel.

181
00:08:39,753 --> 00:08:44,887
About 4,700 bundles, for 392 tubes.

182
00:08:44,982 --> 00:08:47,308
Now the honest part, which you deserve.

183
00:08:47,499 --> 00:08:52,298
After all these years, out of every 100
units of electricity India makes,

184
00:08:52,298 --> 00:08:54,599
only about three come from nuclear.

185
00:08:54,756 --> 00:08:56,827
And were the new units on time?

186
00:08:56,923 --> 00:08:57,445
No.

187
00:08:57,560 --> 00:09:00,810
Kakrapar's first concrete was poured in
2010.

188
00:09:00,920 --> 00:09:03,031
It went critical 10 years later.

189
00:09:03,115 --> 00:09:06,924
You may have read that the government has
approved 10 more, as one fleet.

190
00:09:06,982 --> 00:09:11,731
It said about ₹70,000 crore of orders
would go to Indian industry.

191
00:09:11,798 --> 00:09:14,877
And here is the quiet ending to the old
fight.

192
00:09:14,947 --> 00:09:16,113
Earlier this year,

193
00:09:16,113 --> 00:09:18,669
a Canadian company signed to sell India

194
00:09:18,669 --> 00:09:21,814
nearly 22 million pounds of uranium
concentrate.

195
00:09:21,857 --> 00:09:26,435
That is about 10,000 tonnes, from 2027 to
2035.

196
00:09:26,588 --> 00:09:27,562
Not revenge.

197
00:09:27,630 --> 00:09:29,542
Two countries, trading again.

198
00:09:29,627 --> 00:09:31,048
So what does it tell you?

199
00:09:31,154 --> 00:09:33,318
The fuel, India can buy.

200
00:09:33,359 --> 00:09:36,804
The skill to open its own reactor, nobody
could sell.

201
00:09:37,014 --> 00:09:38,583
India had to grow it.

202
00:09:38,782 --> 00:09:40,488
So here is your date to watch.

203
00:09:40,607 --> 00:09:43,675
The day Rajasthan unit 8 goes critical.

204
00:09:43,881 --> 00:09:47,605
When it does, it will stand on the same
ground Canada left.

205
00:09:47,748 --> 00:09:52,049
A machine is truly yours only when you can
open it, and mend it.

206
00:09:52,297 --> 00:09:54,942
Next, I will take you to 7 in the evening,

207
00:09:54,942 --> 00:09:58,594
when India's lights come on and the grid
holds its breath.

208
00:09:58,802 --> 00:10:00,770
Subscribe, so you are there with me.

209
00:10:00,931 --> 00:10:02,217
And tell me in the comments.

210
00:10:02,306 --> 00:10:05,308
Would you have waited for help, or opened
it yourself?

211
00:10:05,498 --> 00:10:07,270
This is Be Practical with Kishore.

212
00:10:07,490 --> 00:10:08,418
Stay practical.
